  <running_head>INVOCAT. SANCT.</running_head>
  <bottom_margin>
    <catchword>Et</catchword>
    <signature>M 2</signature>
  </bottom_margin>
  <main_text>
    <l>Benedictionem dictam:</l>
    <l>Suspendamus Prophetiam</l>
    <l>De laesione gladii</l>
    <l>Jam tempore gaudii.</l>
    <l>Gaude Christi victoria</l>
    <l>Figitur in memoria</l>
    <l>Cum Leo de tribu Juda</l>
    <l>Surgit a mundi scoria.</l>
    <l>Contra Gabaon Sol stetit,</l>
    <l>Cum Christus victor extitit,</l>
    <l>Phlegethontis antra trivit,</l>
    <l>Dum a morte resurrexit.</l>
    <l>Gaudent supernorum cives,</l>
    <l>Quando natus tuus dives</l>
    <l>Mox aethera transfilivit,</l>
    <l>Arcemq; coeli transcendit.</l>
    <l>Tibi o virgo Maria</l>
    <l>Ingentia tunc gaudia</l>
    <l>Per filium sunt impressa,</l>
    <l>Qui transvolavit aethera.</l>
    <l>Gaude virgo, quatuor seni</l>
    <l>Tibi psallunt laude pleni</l>
    <l>In ascensu tuo leni,</l>
    <l>Ad coelos, dic mihi, veni.</l>
    <l>Ad tam dulcia gaudia</l>
    <l>Atque sancta cantica,</l>
    <l>Sic nos barathri squalida</l>
    <l>Non trucident per secula.</l>
    <l>Amen.</l>
  </main_text>
  <notes>Printed text is set as verse lines; the first word of several lines has decorative initial letters. Some letters in the scan are damaged, notably "Phlegethontis" and "Sic nos barathri", and "Arcemq;" and "Ad tam" may be slightly off. Lines are ended with the printed punctuation as read.</notes>
